module gunbc.host_resource

import std.types { String, Int, Bool }
import product.placement_supply { HostIdentity }

data resource_occurrence_subject_decision_note: String = "WHY THIS IS A DISTINCT CARRIER AND NOT std.occurrence_identity (DESIGN §3, decided by reading rather than by assumption). std.occurrence_identity is specifically about SOURCE occurrences: its OccurrenceCategory enumerates CallableOccurrence/TypeOccurrence/ConstructorOccurrence/FieldOccurrence/MethodOccurrence/NamespaceSegmentOccurrence/LexicalValueOccurrence, its carriers are DeclarationOccurrence/ReferenceOccurrence over OccurrenceContainmentPath, and its allocator is coupled through occurrence_id_allocator_advance_to to AuthoredTokenOrdinalSpace — the authored-source token ordering that gates ModuleExposure. A host resource has no authored token ordinal and no containment path, so a fleet allocator sharing that surface would inherit a watermark constraint that means nothing for it. The subject-agnostic residue in that module is only a monotone Int counter, and sharing ONE Int space across two unrelated subjects is not single authority — it is the cross-family comparison DESIGN closed for ContentHash: occurrence_id_eq would answer true on a numeric coincidence between a host resource and a source occurrence, which is exactly the state-space conflation the family grounding row forbids. So a distinct carrier is correct here. The genuinely shared concept — an opaque, allocator-minted identity in a NAMED subject space, never derived from content — is real but not yet extracted; see resource_occurrence_generalization_trigger."

data minted_not_derived_note: String = "Identity is MINTED BY THE DECLARATION, never derived from content, path, or unit name (operator ruling). This is the whole point: a derived key makes a rename indistinguishable from a delete-plus-create unless some key function is clever enough to notice the two are the same thing — which is inference, and DESIGN §4 rules a heuristic never necessary in a closed system. Git does not track renames; it INFERS them from content similarity after the fact, and that inference is precisely what a content-or-path-derived key_of reproduces. Minting the occurrence at declaration time closes it: the occurrence is stable across every value change, so a rename is ONE MemberChanged whose from and to share an identity, and nothing has to be guessed. A consequence worth stating because it is easy to lose: the identity is NOT the role and NOT the address — role is a separate typed field, and unit name, FIFO path, storage path and configuration are all mutable VALUE fields that may change under a fixed identity."

type ResourceOccurrenceId {
  value: Int
}

type ResourceOccurrenceAllocator {
  next_id: Int
}

type ResourceOccurrenceAllocResult {
  id: ResourceOccurrenceId
  alloc: ResourceOccurrenceAllocator
}

fn resource_occurrence_allocator_initial() -> ResourceOccurrenceAllocator {
  ResourceOccurrenceAllocator { next_id: 0 }
}

fn alloc_resource_occurrence(alloc: ResourceOccurrenceAllocator) -> ResourceOccurrenceAllocResult {
  ResourceOccurrenceAllocResult {
    id: ResourceOccurrenceId { value: alloc.next_id },
    alloc: ResourceOccurrenceAllocator { next_id: alloc.next_id + 1 },
  }
}

fn resource_occurrence_eq(left: ResourceOccurrenceId, right: ResourceOccurrenceId) -> Bool {
  left.value == right.value
}

type HostResourceIdentity {
  host: HostIdentity
  occurrence: ResourceOccurrenceId
}

fn host_resource_identity_eq(left: HostResourceIdentity, right: HostResourceIdentity) -> Bool {
  (left.host as String) == (right.host as String)
    && resource_occurrence_eq(left: left.occurrence, right: right.occurrence)
}

data host_resource_role_parameter_note: String = "role is a TYPE PARAMETER R, not a closed HostRole enum (operator ruling: 'not a closed operator-maintained HostRole enum'). Jobserver, BuildCache and RunnerSlot are typed roles supplied by whichever module owns them, so a new role is a new instantiation rather than an edit to a central coproduct — the same rule DESIGN §3 states for browser products ('adding Opera must not widen a generic browser-product enum'). A central enum would also make the role list an operator maintenance duty, which is the governance redundancy §2 prices."

type HostResource<R, V> {
  identity: HostResourceIdentity
  role: R
  value: V
}

fn host_resource_identity_of<R, V>(resource: HostResource<R, V>) -> HostResourceIdentity {
  resource.identity
}

fn host_resource_same_occurrence<R, V>(
  left: HostResource<R, V>,
  right: HostResource<R, V>,
) -> Bool {
  host_resource_identity_eq(left: left.identity, right: right.identity)
}

data resource_occurrence_generalization_trigger: String = "OPEN, deliberately not built here: the shared concept behind ResourceOccurrenceId and std.occurrence_identity's OccurrenceId is 'an opaque allocator-minted identity in a named subject space'. Extracting it as one subject-parameterized carrier (the Vendor<Domain> move — one concept, the subject as a type argument) is the §2-horizontal answer and would make cross-subject comparison unwritable rather than merely unlikely. It is NOT done in this change because std.occurrence_identity is load-bearing compiler substrate with staged work in flight (the stage0 roster and ContainmentPath<OccurrenceId> projection), so migrating it under a fleet-membership brief would be exactly the improvising past doubt the working agreement forbids. DISSOLVE-ON: a THIRD minted-identity subject appears, or the std.occurrence_identity stage0 projection settles — whichever comes first; at that point extract the parameterized carrier and re-ground both subjects on it."
